# XBD 12.1 Utility Argument Syntax
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The SYNOPSIS notation itself is a convention for readers and applications
# (see the .cov file). What the system must do is stated for option-arguments
# (the same argument or the next one, and an optional option-argument given
# alone) and for numeric values. The tests use the same representative set of
# utilities as XBD 12.2 and run in the POSIX locale.

assertion  XBD.12.1-utility-argument-syntax.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.1
class      required
since      1992
says       An option with a mandatory option-argument is also accepted with
           the option-argument in the same argument as the option, with no
           blank between them (cut -f1, sort -k1,1, head -n1, tail -n1, grep
           -epattern, sed -e'script', mkdir -m755), and the effect is that of
           the two-argument form.
test       t/001-adjacent-option-argument.sh

assertion  XBD.12.1-utility-argument-syntax.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.1
class      required
since      2001
says       A numeric option-argument is read as a decimal integer, so 08 is
           eight and 010 is ten, and a value up to 2147483647 is recognised:
           fold -w 08, head -n 010, fold -w 2147483647 and cut -f 2147483647
           behave as the value says.
test       t/002-numeric-arguments.sh

assertion  XBD.12.1-utility-argument-syntax.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.1
class      required
since      2001
says       When an option whose option-argument is optional is given alone
           (pr -e, pr -n), it behaves as if the option-argument were omitted
           and the next argument is not taken as its option-argument: pr -t
           -e FILE expands tabs in FILE and pr -t -n FILE numbers its lines.
test       t/003-optional-option-argument.sh

